For integers \(B,d\ge1\) with \(m=Bd\) and \(2m\le n\), write \(p=1-(1-q)^d\) and name the testing amplitude
\[
h_\circ(B,d,q)=\frac1{100\pi}
\begin{cases}
\min\{1,d/\sqrt{mp}\},&p>0,\\
1,&p=0.
\end{cases}
\]
The testing handle is the pair of priors
\((\Pi_{+1,h_\circ},\Pi_{-1,h_\circ})\) from Definition \(\mathrm{def:block-prior}\), together with their complete original-record mixture laws \((P_{+1,h_\circ},P_{-1,h_\circ})\). The record includes detailed retained arrows and source labels in \(H\), every treatment coordinate in \(Z\), all noiseless outcomes \(Y(Z)\), repeated-outcome recipient grouping, remaining capacities \(u_\ell\), and the observed global hidden treated-source count \(K\). The conditional law is the coefficient-constrained \(\lambda_{\sigma,h}\) of Lemma \(\mathrm{lem:block-law}\), including its \(M=0\) convention. Support, causal target separation, and the uniform nonlocal testing bound at this amplitude are conclusions of Lemma \(\mathrm{lem:block-law}\) and Theorem \(\mathrm{thm:block-testing-scale}\). Lemma \(\mathrm{lem:hidden-allocation-contraction}\) supplies the all-degree Walsh contraction. These are proved properties of the named construction, rather than additional assumptions. They deliver the scale \(F\), its equivalent positive-retention elbow, the clipped-or-zero rule \(T^\star_{n,d,q}\), and the two consistency criteria stated in the precision theorem.
